WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.322
A norm assigns a vector a size, generalizing ordinary Euclidean length

00:00:05.322 --> 00:00:12.930
A Banach space is a normed vector space in which every Cauchy sequence converges within the space

00:00:12.930 --> 00:00:18.300
Completeness prevents sequences from approaching a missing point outside the chosen system.

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:23.365
In the Euclidean plane, coordinatewise completeness gives this property

00:00:23.365 --> 00:00:30.477
These vectors approach the origin, and their distance from it is square root five divided by n

00:00:30.477 --> 00:00:36.333
That explicit bound proves convergence rather than relying on a few arrows becoming small.

00:00:37.633 --> 00:00:44.544
A Hilbert space also has an inner product generating its norm and is complete for that norm

00:00:44.544 --> 00:00:50.235
In real L two on the unit interval, inner products integrate products of functions

00:00:50.235 --> 00:00:54.300
This makes geometry such as orthogonality available for function vectors.

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:59.053
Not every Banach norm comes from an inner product

00:00:59.053 --> 00:01:07.879
With the maximum-coordinate norm and the two coordinate unit vectors, the parallelogram identity gives two on the left and four on the right

00:01:07.879 --> 00:01:12.100
This complete normed plane is therefore not Hilbert under that norm.
